# UFS clock scaling off on this phone, and it is an EXPERIMENT, not a setting.
#
# WHAT WAS MEASURED (2026-09-12, captured while the phone was frozen; the full
# capture is in tasks/021-live-capture-2026-09-12.md):
#
#     /proc/pressure/io   some avg10=99.12    /proc/pressure/cpu   0.00
#
#     kworker/0:2+events        blk_execute_rq <- ufshcd_exec_dev_cmd
#                               <- ufshcd_query_attr
#                               <- ufshcd_exception_event_handler
#     kworker/u32:0+devfreq_wq  ufshcd_devfreq_scale <- devfreq_monitor
#     kworker/u32:3+flush-7:0   ext4_writepages, jbd2, fsync: all behind it
#
# The device raised an exception event, the query that asks it what happened
# never returned, and clock scaling blocked behind that. With the queue held,
# every write in the system stopped -- including the black box's own fsync,
# which is why fifteen hours of freeze left nothing on disk.
#
# WHY SCALING IS THE SUSPECT. At boot the driver says:
#
#     ufshcd-qcom 1d84000.ufshc: freq-table-hz property not specified
#
# The device tree never gives this controller a frequency table, and devfreq
# was nonetheless running simple_ondemand and sitting in a 50 MHz gear. Scaling
# to gears the hardware was never told about is a good explanation for a
# controller that stops answering.
#
# HOW TO READ THE RESULT. If the freezes stop, this is the cause and the real
# fix is a freq-table-hz in the device tree, not this file. If they do not, this
# is ruled out and the next suspect is auto_hibern8 (150 ms here, longer in the
# vendor kernel). Either way the answer is worth more than the guess.
#
# It has to be set BEFORE a freeze: writing this attribute takes the same lock
# the stuck path holds, so during a freeze the write hangs too.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="scsi_host", DRIVERS=="ufshcd", ATTR{clkscale_enable}=="1", ATTR{clkscale_enable}="0"
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="platform", KERNEL=="*.ufshc", ATTR{clkscale_enable}=="1", ATTR{clkscale_enable}="0"
